    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Coalbed fires refer to underground coal seam fires that consume significant volumes of coal per year in hundreds of fires around the world. These fires often start near coal outcrops where oxygen is abundant. As fires develop, they migrate away from the outcrop into the subsurface formation. At well-developed fires, fissures that connect the surface to the subsurface coal seam are often observed. These fissures form when pre- existing fractures in the region widen as the coal is consumed and subsidence occurs. Fissures play an important role in sustaining combustion by providing pathways for oxygen influx and allowing outflow of exhaust gases. Four different types of fissures—which arise due to different subsurface mechanics—molehill, plateau, narrow and gaping fissures, are observed over a coal fire, called the North Coal Fire, near Durango, CO. Apertures of surface fissures can be expressed as a fraction of the magnitude of subsurface subsidence by using a boundary element model (BEM). Simulation results are expressed using a set of governing dimensionless length scale variables that can be easily measured or estimated at the North Coal Fire. These simulated relationships can be compared to field measurements performed at the North Coal Fire.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
